## 9. Make the "Recent Activity" feed actually live

**This is also a real gap, not polish — flagged separately because it's slightly more involved than a five-minute fix, but it's a well-defined, bounded change, not a big one.**

Today, `app/app/page.tsx`'s Recent Activity Feed section renders the static `ACTIVITY` array imported directly from `mock/data.ts` — it never changes no matter what the user actually does in a session. Separately (same root cause, found while investigating this), `lib/store.ts` already defines a `pushNotification` action that has **zero call sites anywhere in the app** — the plumbing to push a live notification on a real action exists and was never wired up either. Fix both together since they're the same pattern applied to two lists.

### 9.1 Add live activity storage
**File:** `lib/workspace-store.ts`

Add to the store, following the exact shape `ActivityItem` already has in `mock/data.ts` (kind/title/detail/createdAt/href):

```ts
activity: ActivityItem[]
pushActivity: (item: Omit<ActivityItem, 'id' | 'createdAt'>) => void
```

Seed `activity: ACTIVITY` in initial state (same seeding pattern every other list already uses — `bots: BOTS`, `runs: BACKTEST_RUNS`, etc.). Implement `pushActivity` by prepending a new entry with a generated `id` (`slugId('act')` from `lib/utils.ts`, matching the ID convention used everywhere else) and `createdAt: nowISO()` — check `lib/workspace-store.ts` for the exact `nowISO()`-equivalent helper already in use elsewhere in this file (it's used in `addRun`, `setBotStatus`, etc. — reuse it, don't hand-roll a new `new Date().toISOString()` call).

### 9.2 Wire it into real actions — five call sites, all straightforward

Call **both** `pushActivity` and the already-existing-but-unused `pushNotification` at each of these points (they serve different surfaces — the dashboard feed and the notification bell/center — and both are meant to fire on the same real events per the original product spec):

1. **Backtest completes** — `addRun` in `lib/workspace-store.ts` (~line 346). After a run is added: `kind: 'backtest'`, title referencing `run.botName`, detail with the headline metric, `href: /app/bots/${run.botId}/backtest/${run.id}`.
2. **Node unlocked** — `components/builder/unlock-dialog.tsx`, both the credit-spend and the direct-purchase success paths (~lines 64 and 71, right next to the existing `toast.unlock(...)` calls). `kind: 'unlock'`, title naming the component.
3. **Preset published** — `publishPreset` in `lib/workspace-store.ts` (~line 454). `kind: 'publish'`, title naming the preset, `href: /app/marketplace/${preset.id}`.
4. **Bot promoted to live** — every `setBotStatus(bot.id, 'live')` call site (`components/backtest/results-actions.tsx`, `app/app/page.tsx`, `app/app/live/page.tsx` — three sites, all doing the same state transition). `kind: 'live'`, title naming the bot.
5. **Credits purchased / plan changed** — wherever `addCredits`/`setPlan` are called from the billing pages (`app/app/billing/topup/page.tsx`, `app/app/billing/page.tsx`, `app/app/billing/plans/page.tsx`). `kind: 'payment'`.

For each, write a title/detail string in the same voice as the existing seeded `ACTIVITY` entries in `mock/data.ts` — read a few of those first so the newly-generated ones don't read differently from the seeded history sitting above them in the same feed.

### 9.3 Read from the store, not the static import
**File:** `app/app/page.tsx`

Replace `import { ACTIVITY } from '@/mock/data'` + the direct `.map()` over it with `const activity = useWorkspace((s) => s.activity)` and map over that instead. The seeded mock history stays visible (it's still in the array, just now it's the store's initial state instead of a hardcoded import), and new real actions will appear above it, newest first, without a refresh.

### 9.4 Scope discipline for this section
Don't wire every possible action — the five above cover the meaningful, already-spec'd `ActivityItem['kind']` values (`backtest`, `edit`, `unlock`, `publish`, `live`, `payment` — six kinds exist in the type; `edit` is deliberately left out here, since firing an activity entry on every single node/config edit would flood the feed with noise no real product would want; leave `edit`-kind entries as seed-only, static history, not a live trigger).

